.TH MAILX 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
mailx \- send and receive local mail
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mailx
.RB [ \-eHiNn ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR subject ]
.I recipient
.RI [ recipient ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B mailx
.RB [ \-eHiNn ]
.RB [ \-u
.IR user ]
.br
.B mailx
.B \-f
.RB [ \-eHiNn ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mailx
sends mail (Send Mode, the first
.B SYNOPSIS
form above, chosen whenever at least one
.I recipient
operand is given) or reads mail (Receive Mode, the other two forms).
.PP
This implementation performs real local delivery only: it appends
composed messages directly onto a real mbox-format file on this same
machine, and there is no mail-transfer agent for it to hand a message
to for network relay. There is also exactly one local user this
process can ever run as; every
.I recipient
must name that user (any \(dq@host\(dq suffix is accepted but
ignored, since there is no host to resolve against). Any other
recipient is refused with a \(dquser unknown\(dq diagnostic, the same
way a real mail-transfer agent bounces mail addressed to a name it
does not know, rather than silently discarding it.
.SS Send Mode
The message body is read verbatim from standard input up to
end-of-file; there is no in-body command language (no \(dq~.\(dq-style
escapes) to end a message early. If
.B \-s
is not given and standard input is a terminal, a \(dqSubject:\ \(dq
prompt is read interactively; if standard input is not a terminal,
the message is sent with no Subject header at all.
.SS Receive Mode
Opens a mailbox (the system mailbox by default, the file named by
.B \-f
with
.BR \-f ,
or the mailbox named indirectly through
.B \-u
otherwise), prints a one-line summary of every message unless
.B \-N
was given, and, unless
.B \-H
was given or the mailbox could only be opened read-only, enters an
interactive command loop reading commands from standard input. Each
prompt is \(dq&\ \(dq. A
.I msglist
argument to a command below is a bare message number,
.B *
(every message),
.B $
(the last message), or empty (meaning the current message); message
ranges and arithmetic
.RI ( n\-m ,
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.BR ^ )
are not implemented.
.TP
.BR p " [msglist], " t " [msglist]"
Print the given message(s) (or the current message, if
.I msglist
is empty), then advance the current message. This is also what a bare
message number, or an empty input line, does.
.TP
.BR n " [msglist]"
Print the next message after the current one.
.TP
.BR d " [msglist]"
Mark the given message(s) deleted.
.TP
.BR u " [msglist]"
Unmark the given message(s) as deleted.
.TP
.B h
Redisplay the header summary.
.TP
.B q
Rewrite the mailbox in place, keeping every message not marked
deleted, and exit.
.TP
.BR x ", " ex ", " exit
Exit without rewriting the mailbox; any pending deletions are
discarded.
.TP
.B =
Print the current message number.
.TP
.B ?
Print a short command summary.
.TP
.BR # " comment"
A comment; the rest of the line is ignored.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-e
Test for the presence of new mail in the system mailbox and exit;
nothing is printed. See
.B EXIT STATUS
below.
.TP
.B \-f
Enter Receive Mode reading
.I file
if given, or the secondary mailbox
.RB ( $MBOX ,
see
.BR ENVIRONMENT )
otherwise, instead of the system mailbox.
.TP
.B \-H
In Receive Mode, print only the header summary and exit; do not enter
the interactive command loop.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore the
.B SIGINT
signal for the duration of the run.
.TP
.B \-N
Suppress the header summary that Receive Mode would otherwise print
before the command loop starts.
.TP
.B \-n
Accepted as a documented no-op. On systems that read a start-up file
of user mail defaults,
.B \-n
suppresses that read; this implementation never reads such a file
regardless of this flag, so there is nothing left for it to disable.
.TP
.BI \-s " subject"
Set the message's Subject header in Send Mode. With no
.BR \-s ,
see
.B "Send Mode"
above for how the Subject header (if any) is determined.
.TP
.BI \-u " user"
In Receive Mode, read
.IR user 's
mailbox instead of the caller's own. Refused unless
.I user
names the one real local user (there being no other mailbox this
implementation could ever read).
.TP
.B \-F
Recognized but always refused: a real
.B mailx
uses
.B \-F
to additionally record outgoing mail into a file named after the
first recipient, which this implementation does not do.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I recipient
The name of the mail recipient, in Send Mode. Must name the one real
local user this process runs as.
.TP
.I file
In Receive Mode with
.BR \-f ,
the mailbox file to read. If omitted, the secondary mailbox is used
instead.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MAIL
Pathname of the system mailbox (Send Mode's delivery target, and
Receive Mode's default source). If unset or empty, defaults to
.BR mailbox
in the current user's home directory.
.TP
.B MBOX
Pathname of the secondary mailbox that
.B \-f
with no
.I file
operand reads. If unset or empty, defaults to
.BR mbox
in the current user's home directory.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
A message body line that itself begins with the literal text
\(dqFrom\ \(dq (as a sender might type it) is escaped on write by
prefixing a
.BR > ,
matching mbox format's own rule for keeping such lines from being
mistaken for a message boundary; this escaping is not reversed on
read, so a line that was really typed as \(dq>From\ \(dq and one
produced by this escaping are indistinguishable afterward.
This is a known, long-standing limitation of the traditional mbox
format, not specific to this implementation.
.PP
Concurrent delivery is safe: every append and every mailbox rewrite
holds an exclusive lock on the mailbox file for its whole duration, so
two
.B mailx
processes acting on the same mailbox at once cannot interleave their
writes.
.PP
Quitting out of the system mailbox with
.B q
does not migrate read-but-kept messages into the secondary mailbox
the way some historical implementations do; it always rewrites
whichever mailbox file was actually opened, in place, dropping only
the messages marked deleted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The message was sent successfully (Send Mode); the command loop was
exited normally, or the header summary was printed successfully
(Receive Mode); or, with
.BR \-e ,
new mail is present in the system mailbox.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred (a recipient other than the one real local user, a
mailbox that could not be opened or is not a valid mbox file, and so
on); or, with
.BR \-e ,
no new mail is present.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1)
